From 487bd892c035ec7a6948f0392107db56a95da526 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stefanoconiglio <33290924+stefanoconiglio@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 06:34:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] A picture cut by \includegraphics[trim=...,clip] is
 drawn cut, as XeTeX draws it

xetex.def writes a bitmap as "pdf:image bbox llx lly urx ury clip 1
width W (file)": the bbox, in bp of the picture, is the part shown, and
fills W. The bbox was read as x0 x1 y0 y1 and never used: the whole
picture was squeezed into the cut's size. embed_image now draws it as
dvipdfmx's scale_to_fit_I does, the whole picture scaled so the bbox
fills width x height, clipped to it when clip is 1.

A picture's size in bp is XeTeX's: a picture that gives no resolution
in absolute units (no pHYs in metres, no JFIF density in dpi or dpcm)
is 72 dpi to XeTeX, 96 to mupdf, so its image is set to 72 dpi when
loaded.

A PDF picture is cut by "x:gsave", a "pdf:literal ... re W n" clip,
the picture and "x:grestore", inside the pdf:btrans and pdf:etrans of
its scaling. Since pdf:literal is drawn, the clip opened and was never
closed: x:gsave and x:grestore were not known, and pdf:etrans gave
back the graphics state without popping the clips opened since
pdf:btrans; the draw device then refused to close and TeXpresso died.
x:gsave and x:grestore are now q and Q, and pdf:etrans pops its clips
as Q does.
